1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,738
Before, you'd waste time watching your AI think out loud;

2
00:00:03,738 --> 00:00:06,465
now Inkling 2 Mini thinks more in its head,

3
00:00:06,465 --> 00:00:08,066
so you wait less, but how?

4
00:00:08,566 --> 00:00:10,664
Here's how, in one hard exam question.

5
00:00:11,164 --> 00:00:12,422
You ask a hard question.

6
00:00:12,722 --> 00:00:15,053
An ordinary model, like Gemma or Qwen,

7
00:00:15,053 --> 00:00:17,205
thinks by writing: line after line,

8
00:00:17,205 --> 00:00:18,939
and you wait for every line.

9
00:00:19,439 --> 00:00:21,442
Same question on Thinking Machines'

10
00:00:21,442 --> 00:00:23,780
Inkling 2 Mini, out November 20:

11
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:26,061
it does more of the working in its head,

12
00:00:26,061 --> 00:00:27,508
then writes a short note.

13
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:28,441
How?

14
00:00:28,741 --> 00:00:31,855
Its middle 12 layers run 3 times, same weights:

15
00:00:31,855 --> 00:00:35,526
48 layer steps from 24 layers, no bigger brain.

16
00:00:36,026 --> 00:00:38,083
In the maker's test on a research twin,

17
00:00:38,083 --> 00:00:40,603
three passes wrote less than half the reasoning.

18
00:00:40,903 --> 00:00:44,413
And it's open: about 20 gigabytes, for a 32 gig Mac.

19
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:47,452
That's how you wait less: less thinking to write out.

20
00:00:47,752 --> 00:00:50,232
The catch: each word it writes takes more work,

21
00:00:50,232 --> 00:00:53,228
so speed depends on your setup, and it still writes some.

22
00:00:53,728 --> 00:00:56,385
Same exam; more of the working happens in its head.

23
00:00:56,885 --> 00:00:58,287
Subscribe for the next one.
